---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = "What to do after failing first year engineering in India: backlog rules, branch change and eight routes ranked by growth";
const seoTitle = "After Failing First Year Engineering: Backlogs, Branch Change, Routes";
const description = "Failed first year engineering? See how backlog and promotion rules differ, when branch change is possible, and eight routes ranked by growth.";
const seoDescription = "Failed first year engineering? See how backlog rules differ, when branch change works, and eight routes ranked by growth.";
const canonical = "https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/what-to-do-after-failing-first-year-engineering-india/";
const publishedDateISO = "2026-10-30";
const publishedDateLabel = "30 October 2026";
const authorName = "Allu Vagdevi";
const authorUrl = "https://futurecareerschool.com/about/allu-vagdevi/";
const authorRole = "Career researcher and writer";

const faqItems = [
  {
    "q": "What should I do if I fail first year engineering?",
    "a": "Find out which rule applies to you, because colleges differ. If you are promoted with backlogs, plan a schedule to clear them. If you are detained, ask exactly which courses you must repeat, and set a date to decide whether to stay, switch or leave."
  },
  {
    "q": "How many backlogs are allowed to move to second year?",
    "a": "It depends on your college and university. One college's examination cell page allows moving to the second year with no more than eight failed subjects across the first two semesters, with no more than five from end-semester exams. Another college's rules say a student who does not earn 50 percent of the previous year's credits loses one academic year."
  },
  {
    "q": "Can I change my branch if I have a backlog?",
    "a": "Usually not. Reporting on the AKTU 2026-27 session says students must have passed all first-year subjects in the first attempt and hold at least a 6.5 CGPA, with a vacant seat. Other universities and colleges set their own rules."
  },
  {
    "q": "Is it better to repeat the year or leave engineering?",
    "a": "Repeat if the cause is fixable and you still want the degree. Consider leaving if the branch does not fit and you have a clear alternative, such as a diploma, a different degree or a skill route. Test the choice against time, money and the 4-Checkpoint Protocol."
  },
  {
    "q": "Can I join a diploma after failing engineering?",
    "a": "Diplomas usually ask for Class 10, so it may be possible. A diploma holder can later enter the second year of B.Tech through lateral entry, which the AICTE chairman described as a provision, though seats and rules differ by state."
  },
  {
    "q": "Will a backlog affect my job chances?",
    "a": "It can, because some companies ask for no active backlogs at the time of campus selection. Check each company's eligibility line and clear backlogs before placement season where you can."
  },
  {
    "q": "How long can I take to finish a B.Tech?",
    "a": "Colleges set a maximum. MITAOE's rules, for example, allow up to twelve semesters for an eight-semester programme, within a credit requirement. Ask your college for its limit."
  },
  {
    "q": "What if I failed because of attendance?",
    "a": "Rules vary. One college's rules describe course-wise detention for low attendance and re-registration for the course in the next academic year. Ask the exam cell which options apply to you."
  },
  {
    "q": "Is it possible to build a good career after failing first year?",
    "a": "Yes. Employers in many technical fields care about skills and proof. A failed year is a setback, and a cleared backlog plus a visible project is a strong base."
  },
  {
    "q": "Who can I talk to if I feel overwhelmed?",
    "a": "Start with a counsellor, a teacher or a family member you trust. Tele-MANAS (14416) is also listed on college pages as a Government of India helpline."
  }
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>After failing first year engineering in India, your next step depends on your college rulebook: some students move up with backlogs, others repeat courses or a year, and a backlog can close the door to a branch change.</strong> It is a setback, not the end of a technical career. The decisions that unlock high income opportunities start with clearing the pass and building a high-income skill portfolio people can see.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Read your exact rule first.</strong> Colleges differ on how many backlogs you can carry, and the rulebook decides whether you move up or repeat.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose stay, switch or leave by a date.</strong> A backlog usually closes branch change, so plan around clearing it and building skills.</li>
      <li><strong>Build proof beside the degree.</strong> One finished project is the start of a skill portfolio and a faster path to financial freedom.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide is part of our set of <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options for every stream and degree</a>. If you have cleared the backlogs and want to look ahead, read <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-btech/">career options after B.Tech</a>. For school-level setbacks, see <a href="/blog/career-options/what-to-do-after-failing-class-12-india/">what to do after failing Class 12</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      <li><a href="#which">Which situation are you in?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#rules">How promotion rules differ</a></li>
      <li><a href="#two-weeks">The first two weeks</a></li>
      <li><a href="#clear">Staying and clearing it</a></li>
      <li><a href="#branch">Changing branch</a></li>
      <li><a href="#routes">Eight routes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#money">Money and fees</a></li>
      <li><a href="#checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</a></li>
      <li><a href="#gates">The 3 Gates</a></li>
      <li><a href="#mistakes">Expensive mistakes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#next-step">What to do next</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">FAQs</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="which">Which situation are you in?</h2>

  <p>
    "Failed first year" can mean very different things. Your next step depends on which of these four you are in, and your own college rulebook decides which one it is.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Situation</th>
          <th>What it usually means</th>
          <th>First move</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Situation"><strong>Backlogs, but promoted</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it usually means">You carry a few failed subjects into second year and clear them in later exams, within the limit your college allows.</td>
          <td data-label="First move">Register for the backlog exams at the first chance and plan the clearing order.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Situation"><strong>Detained or short on credits</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it usually means">You do not meet the promotion rule and repeat the year or the failed courses.</td>
          <td data-label="First move">Ask the exam cell exactly what you must repeat and by when.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Situation"><strong>Failed because of attendance or internal marks</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it usually means">The detention is course-wise, and the end-semester exam may not be open to you.</td>
          <td data-label="First move">Ask what remedial or re-registration option exists for each course.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Situation"><strong>Passed, but the marks are low</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it usually means">No failure, but the branch or the college does not suit you.</td>
          <td data-label="First move">Treat this as a fit problem, not a pass-or-fail problem, and use the routes below.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line">
    Rules checked on 30 October 2026 from college and university documents named in the next section. Your own college and university can differ, so read your own exam regulations.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A failed first year is one of the most common setbacks in engineering, and it is rarely the end of a technical career. What hurts is guessing the rules and finding out in the last week. Get the rulebook answer first, then decide whether to stay, switch or leave.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rules">How promotion rules differ between colleges</h2>

  <p>
    There is no single national rule for carrying backlogs into second year. Two published examples show how far colleges can differ.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>College document</th>
          <th>What it says</th>
          <th>What to learn from it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="College document"><strong>TCET, Mumbai (examination cell page, year not stated)</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it says">For BE, B.Tech and B.Voc, a student may move into the second year by passing every course in Semesters I and II, or by failing no more than eight subjects in the two semesters, with no more than five of those failures from end-semester exams.</td>
          <td data-label="What to learn from it">Some colleges allow a fairly large number of backlogs. The page does not say what happens above the limit, so ask.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="College document"><strong>MITAOE, Pune (B.Tech examination rules, 2024-25)</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it says">To move up, a student must earn 50 percent of the previous year's credits. Those who do not "will consequently lose one academic year". Failed courses are repeated, and a student may take up to twelve semesters to finish an eight-semester programme.</td>
          <td data-label="What to learn from it">Credit-based rules turn a failed first year into a repeat year. A longer maximum duration gives more time, but it costs fees.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Use these as examples only. Look up your university's ordinance and your college's exam manual, and ask the exam cell for the page number that applies to your batch.
  </p>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>What is the maximum number of backlogs for promotion?</li>
    <li>Do internal marks and attendance carry forward if I re-register?</li>
    <li>When are backlog exams held, and what is the extra fee per course?</li>
    <li>What is the maximum time to finish the degree?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="two-weeks">The first two weeks after the result</h2>

  <div class="ladder">
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Read the regulation, not the rumour</div>
        <p>Get the exam manual and ask the exam cell to mark which clause applies to you. Seniors often remember an older rule.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">List every failed course with its reason</div>
        <p>Write down whether each failure was marks, attendance or internal assessment. The fix is different for each.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Tell your family early and show the plan</div>
        <p>A plan with dates and costs calms a conversation faster than an apology.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Decide stay, switch or leave by a date</div>
        <p>Set a date within the next month to decide. Drifting for a semester is the most expensive choice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    If the result weighs on you, say so to a teacher, a counsellor or a family member this week. College support pages also list Tele-MANAS (14416), a Government of India mental health helpline that runs around the clock.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clear">Staying: clearing backlogs without losing the next year</h2>

  <p>
    Most students who stay clear their backlogs, but the ones who do it well treat it like a project with a schedule.
  </p>
  <h3>Build the backlog plan</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Sort failed courses by difficulty and by how many marks you were short.</li>
    <li>Clear the course with the nearest exam first; confidence helps with the harder ones.</li>
    <li>Avoid registering for too many backlog courses along with the current semester.</li>
    <li>Use the remedial term where the college offers one; MITAOE's rules describe a short remedial term for declared courses.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Change how you study, not only how long</h3>
  <p>
    If your first-year method was last-night revision, three more months of it will not change the result. Swap in past papers, a study partner who passed the subject and one weekly doubt session with the faculty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Many first-year failures come from maths and basic engineering courses. Spend a week rebuilding the fundamentals before you attempt the same paper again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="branch">Changing branch: when it is possible</h2>

  <p>
    Branch change is a college and university decision, and it is easier with a clean record. Reporting on the AKTU 2026-27 session says students must have passed all first-year subjects in the first attempt, hold at least a 6.5 CGPA, and need a vacant seat in the branch they want.
  </p>
  <p>
    So a backlog usually closes this door, and the rule is not an automatic right even for those who qualify. The AICTE chairman said in April 2025 that branch changes are getting harder because of high demand for computer science.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you already have a backlog, do not plan your year around a branch change. Plan around clearing the backlog and building skills in the branch you have.
  </p>

  <h2 id="routes">Eight routes after a failed first year, strongest growth first</h2>

  <p>
    The routes are ordered by how far visible skill can lift income. The rank-based routes sit last.
  </p>
  <div class="route-grid">
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">01</span>
        <span class="chip chip--high">Scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Stay and build software, data or automation skill beside the degree</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Employers in these fields look at projects and the way you explain them. A repeated year gives you extra months to build proof.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Startups, small firms and clients who buy a finished project; later, product companies.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>A live project, a clear readme and a referral from someone who used it.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Entry-level coding tasks are the ones AI tools absorb first. Aim for work that needs judgement on a real problem.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Pick one stack, build three projects for one industry, then take small paid work. First sales skill: one line saying who your work helps.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">02</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Stay in your branch and add a core technical skill with a business layer</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Electrical, mechanical, civil and electronics students can add skills such as solar, CAD, automation or testing that local businesses pay for.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Contractors, factories and households who need installation, design or repair.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Finished jobs, photographs of the work and customer references.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>A degree in a core branch moves slowly. The business layer is what speeds it up.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Take one small job a month, quote clearly and ask each customer for a referral.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">03</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Switch to a diploma or a related degree</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">A shorter, more practical course can fit some students better. A diploma holder can enter engineering again through lateral entry, which AICTE has described as a provision for the second year.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Technical employers; later, your own work.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Marks, workshop skills and internships.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Lateral-entry seats are limited and rules differ by state. Admission to a diploma usually needs Class 10 results.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Visit the polytechnic and ask about placements and the lateral-entry process before you decide.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">04</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Move to BCA, BSc computer science or another degree</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">If the fit is wrong rather than the ability, a different degree may suit you. Computing skills carry across degrees.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Employers and clients who care about what you can do.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Projects, internships and certificates from known providers.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Do not pay a high fee to escape a problem you have not understood.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Check how many credits or years you would lose and what the new fee is.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">05</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Sales, marketing and operations roles</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Many engineers do well in technical sales and operations because they understand the product. Results are visible numbers.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Manufacturers, software firms and distributors.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Sales numbers, customer references and clear communication.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Commission-only offers that ask you to pay first are a common trap.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Learn one product deeply, track your numbers and follow up on every lead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">06</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Start a small business or service</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Repair, installation, tuition, design and resale can start small and teach pricing and customers.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Local households, shops and small firms.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Repeat orders and word of mouth.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Do not borrow heavily before customers have paid you.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Start with one service, keep a customer list and raise prices as demand grows.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">07</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Design, content and creative technical work</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Technical students can combine drawing, video or writing with domain knowledge, such as explaining tools or building product visuals.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Brands, creators and technical firms.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Three finished pieces for one kind of client.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>AI drafts basic visuals and text now, so your edge is domain knowledge and reliability.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Pick a niche and offer a fixed-price package.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">08</span>
        <span class="chip chip--low">Not so scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Government and public-sector exams</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Engineering qualifies you for technical posts, and selection is by ranked exams. Exam study also transfers to other tests.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Central and state departments and public-sector undertakings.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Your rank and clean documents.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Two hard stages: a competitive written exam and later a limited number of seats. Keep a parallel skill.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Set a review date with your family and keep a skill that earns outside the exam.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path:</strong> a small technical service in your own area, such as repair, installation, CCTV, solar or app development for local shops.
    </p>
    <p>
      Quote the price and what is included for every job. Keep a list of your customers and add a helper when the orders exceed your hours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: sunk cost, extra fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    A failed year adds fees, backlog exam charges and sometimes an extra year. Count the full additional cost before you decide to stay or leave.
  </p>
  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution. A failed year is a good moment to test your current plan against that.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not let the money already spent decide the next step alone. Ask what the next year gives you in skills, proof and income, and compare it with the cheaper alternatives.
  </p>
  <p class="note-line">
    We give no salary figures because we found no dependable source for these routes. Read live job ads and ask people in the work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol: stay, switch or leave</h2>

  <p>
    Test your decision with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>: Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>
  <div class="protocol">
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Biology: why did the marks drop?</div>
        <p>Look at whether the problem was maths, attendance, health, homesickness or a method that stopped working. A fixable cause points to staying.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Context: time, money and home support</div>
        <p>Write down how many extra months and how much extra money the family can carry. Include the cost of an extra year.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Market: what do employers in your branch ask?</div>
        <p>Read ten job notices for your branch and for the branch you might switch to. Note degree, skills and first-job pay.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Survival: what survives AI?</div>
        <p>Routine coding and drawing tasks are the first to be automated. Pick the path that builds judgement, domain knowledge and customer contact.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gates">The 3 Gates: proof before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The 3 Gates</strong> check that your effort turns into work other people rely on.
  </p>
  <div class="gates-block">
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>Build one small working thing in your branch, such as a circuit, a script, a CAD model or a field repair, and keep photos or a short video of it running.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof you can explain it</span>
        <p>Explain how it works to a relative who is not an engineer. If they can say it back, you understand it well enough to sell it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof someone will pay</span>
        <p>Show it to a workshop, a lab or a small firm and ask what a version made for them would be worth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive mistakes after a failed first year</h2>

  <div class="mistake-list">
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Hiding the result</div>
        <p>Backlog exam forms and re-registration have deadlines. Tell one trusted adult early.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Piling on backlog courses</div>
        <p>Registering for too many together with the regular semester sets up a second failure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Switching branch on rumour</div>
        <p>Branch change is not automatic, and a backlog can close it. Read the notice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Leaving without a plan</div>
        <p>Quitting without a route to a pass, a skill or income costs time you cannot recover.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">05</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Paying to escape the problem</div>
        <p>A new college with a high fee does not fix a method that did not work.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Get the rulebook answer in writing, list your failed courses and their reasons, and pick a decision date within the next month. Start one small project in your chosen route beside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A specialist can look at your marks, interests, budget and family situation with you, which cuts the guesswork after a setback. A sensible first step is <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-after-engineering/">career counselling for engineering students</a>. The aim is a technical skill set that pays, and an earlier path to financial freedom, whatever your first-year transcript says.
  </p>

